# Units in a worksheet

A climber of mass $m \equiv 70\,\mathrm{kg}$ stands on Earth, where $g \equiv 9.81\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$.

Her weight is

$$ W := m g $$

$$ W = ? $$

A car drives at $v := 100\,\mathrm{km/h}$ for $t := 12\,\mathrm{s}$.

$$ v \operatorname{in} \mathrm{m/s} = ? $$

$$ v t \operatorname{in} \mathrm{m} = ? $$

The room is at $T := 25\,{}^\circ\mathrm{C}$; in Fahrenheit that is $T \operatorname{in} {}^\circ\mathrm{F} = ?$.

Angles in degrees are fine inside trigonometric functions: $\sin(30^\circ) = ?$.

A $2\,\mathrm{kW}$ heater left on for $3\,\mathrm{h}$ uses $2\,\mathrm{kW} \cdot 3\,\mathrm{h} \operatorname{in} \mathrm{kWh} = ?$.

## Mistakes the checker catches

Adding a length to a time: $3\,\mathrm{m} + t = ?$

An exponential of a length: $\exp(2\,\mathrm{m}) = ?$

A letter is a unit only straight after a number or when it is set upright as a unit (the Units picker does this for you), so $m$ above is still the climber's mass.
